<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scholasticism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as scholastic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scholasticism was the dominant method of philosophy and theology in the universities of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which sought to reconcile Christian doctrine with reason, especially the philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>. The commentaries on Aristotle by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> (Ibn Rushd), written under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> patronage, transformed European scholastic philosophy when translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, influencing thinkers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scholasticism.jpg" alt="Scholasticism" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gautier de Metz, L'image du monde (medieval copy) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Master_and_scholars_-_1464_-_L%27image_du_Monde.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
